// The `u64` limit, named rather than pretended away. Roadmap A7.
//
// A Burxt `Int` is a SIGNED 64-bit integer, so a `u64` splits into two halves and they fail
// differently:
//
//   * a NEGATIVE Int is refused here, at runtime, and that is a real check rather than a
//     formality — `-1` reaching C as a `size_t` is not a small number, it is 18446744073709551615,
//     and every C API that has been handed one has the bug to show for it.
//   * a u64 ABOVE `Int`'s maximum cannot be checked at all, because there is no Int that holds it.
//     The message says so instead of claiming a range the language cannot express.
//
// That asymmetry is why `u64` is the one width whose check is not the narrow-widen-compare round
// trip the other three share: truncating an i64 to 64 bits is a no-op, so the round trip would
// agree with itself and check nothing.
external function malloc(n: u64) -> CPointer;
let mutable size: Int = 0;
for i in 0..1 {
    size = 0 - 1;
}
let block: CPointer = malloc(size);
print(c_is_null(block));
